n64recomp: a place the runtime can take the thread away

Only one recompiled thread runs at a time and the runtime chooses which, but
it only gets to choose when the running thread calls into it. On the console
that choice is made by an interrupt, at any instruction at all: a thread that
waits by reading memory is preempted, the thread that writes the memory runs,
and the wait ends.

`spin_wait` already covers the smallest form of that -- a loop the recompiler
can prove reads memory and does nothing else. Banjo-Tooie has a bigger one.
Between the end of its opening cutscene and the title screen it walks its
sixty sound emitters, stops each one, and goes round again until none is
still playing. That loop calls functions, so the recompiler cannot prove
anything about it, and it never asks the runtime for anything -- so the
retrace never arrives, the audio thread never runs, the sound never finishes,
and the console stops dead with every other thread waiting on a message that
is sitting in a queue nobody will drain.

So: every function entry is a place the thread can be handed over. The check
is a load of a word and a branch that is never taken, and the word is only
set when there is something to hand over to. Any loop that calls anything is
now interruptible, which together with `spin_wait` is every loop that can
wait for another thread.

The status register goes with the call because a game that has turned
interrupts off has said, in the console's own terms, that this is not a
moment it may be interrupted at.

